{"findings":[{"claim_id":"c1","quote":"Declasal outperforms competing antibiotics.","issue":"Cross-study comparison presented as general superiority claim; no head-to-head trial exists, and the two underlying studies differ in population, severity, duration, timepoint, and analysis population.","explanation":"This headline says Declasal beats 'competing antibiotics' in general, but the only comparison available is between two completely different, non-comparable studies of Declasal and one competitor, Zentrimycin. The populations were different (mild outpatient cellulitis vs. hospitalised complicated infections), the treatment courses were different lengths, and even the way 'cure' was measured differed. The label itself states no head-to-head trial has ever been conducted against any other antibacterial agent.","suggested_fix":"Remove the general superiority claim. Either drop the comparative headline entirely or limit it to describing Declasal's own trial results without a competitor comparison, since no valid comparative or head-to-head data exist.","severity":"blocker","concern":"","tags":[],"confidence":92,"citations":[{"source_id":"acme_pharma:reference:ztm-07:2","passage":"No head-to-head trial of Declasal against Zentrimycin has been conducted."},{"source_id":"acme_pharma:label:declasal:v1.0:2","passage":"DECLASAL has not been compared with any other antibacterial agent in a head-to-head trial."}]},{"claim_id":"c2","quote":"Declasal delivered a higher clinical cure rate than Zentrimycin in skin-infection studies.","issue":"Comparison between two non-matching trials (population, severity, duration, timepoint, analysis population all differ), not a head-to-head trial; also compares a clinically evaluable figure to an intent-to-treat figure.","explanation":"The claim implies a fair, direct comparison of cure rates, but the Declasal study enrolled healthier outpatients with mild cellulitis while the Zentrimycin study enrolled sicker, hospitalised patients with complicated infections, and used a shorter course and different analysis population (clinically evaluable vs intent-to-treat). The evidence explicitly says these differences mean a sicker population would show a lower cure rate under any drug, and comparing a clinically evaluable rate to an intent-to-treat rate inflates the gap further.","suggested_fix":"Remove the cross-trial comparison. State each product's own trial results separately without claiming one is superior, or commission a head-to-head trial before making this claim.","severity":"blocker","concern":"","tags":[],"confidence":95,"citations":[{"source_id":"acme_pharma:reference:ztm-07:2","passage":"A sicker, hospitalised population will record a lower cure rate under any agent. Placing 92.4% beside 68% also sets a clinically evaluable figure against an intent-to-treat figure, which overstates the gap a second time."},{"source_id":"acme_pharma:reference:ztm-07:2","passage":"No head-to-head trial of Declasal against Zentrimycin has been conducted."}]},{"claim_id":"c3","quote":"Choose the more effective antibiotic.","issue":"Implies direct, validated superiority over Zentrimycin based on non-comparable studies; no head-to-head trial exists.","explanation":"This call-to-action tells readers to pick Declasal because it's 'more effective,' but that conclusion rests on comparing two studies that tested very different kinds of patients under different conditions. Without a head-to-head trial, this persuasive claim isn't actually proven.","suggested_fix":"Remove or rephrase to avoid declaring overall superiority; focus on Declasal's own demonstrated efficacy in its studied population instead.","severity":"blocker","concern":"","tags":[],"confidence":90,"citations":[{"source_id":"acme_pharma:reference:ztm-07:2","passage":"No head-to-head trial of Declasal against Zentrimycin has been conducted."}]},{"claim_id":"c4","quote":"Declasal 92%","issue":"Uses the clinically evaluable population figure (92.4%) rather than the modified intent-to-treat figure (89.1%) that is the basis of the label claim, without disclosing this is a subgroup/analysis-population figure, and presents it as the general cure rate.","explanation":"The chart shows '92%' as if it's simply Declasal's cure rate, but the actual primary efficacy result for the full study population (modified intent-to-treat) was 89.1%; 92.4% only reflects patients who stuck exactly to the protocol, which is a higher, less representative number. This swaps in the more favorable number without noting it is not the overall result.","suggested_fix":"Use the modified intent-to-treat figure (89.1%) as the headline result, or clearly label 92% as the clinically evaluable population result rather than the general clinical cure rate.","severity":"blocker","concern":"","tags":[],"confidence":85,"citations":[{"source_id":"acme_pharma:reference:dcl-301:1","passage":"Clinical cure, modified intent-to-treat population (n=412): 89.1%.
Clinical cure, clinically evaluable population (n=383): 92.4%.

The two figures describe the same endpoint at the same visit in two analysis populations. The clinically evaluable population excludes patients who deviated from the protocol, so it is the higher of the two and is not the population on which the label claim rests."}]},{"claim_id":"c5","quote":"Zentrimycin 68%","issue":"Presented side-by-side with Declasal's 92% as a comparable figure, but drawn from a different, non-matching study (different population, severity, duration, timepoint, and intent-to-treat vs clinically evaluable analysis population).","explanation":"Putting Zentrimycin's 68% next to Declasal's 92% in the same chart makes it look like an apples-to-apples comparison, but the Zentrimycin number comes from sicker, hospitalised patients assessed differently and using an intent-to-treat analysis, while Declasal's number is from healthier outpatients using a clinically evaluable analysis. The chart itself invites a conclusion the underlying studies cannot support.","suggested_fix":"Do not display the two figures in a single comparative chart. If both results must be shown, present them separately with a clear statement that they come from non-comparable trials and cannot be used to infer relative efficacy.","severity":"blocker","concern":"","tags":[],"confidence":92,"citations":[{"source_id":"acme_pharma:reference:ztm-07:2","passage":"A sicker, hospitalised population will record a lower cure rate under any agent. Placing 92.4% beside 68% also sets a clinically evaluable figure against an intent-to-treat figure, which overstates the gap a second time."}]}]}
